<DocsLayout title="Technology stack" subtitle="Versions and responsibilities taken from workspace manifests and runtime configuration." description="Versions and responsibilities taken from workspace manifests and runtime configuration." toc={toc}>
  <section id="runtime" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Runtime and service framework</h2>
    <p>The root manifest selects <code>bun@1.4.3</code> as the package manager. Bun installs the workspaces, runs <code>bun:test</code>, type checks with the built-in <code>bun check</code>, serves the sidecar, provides SQLite and password hashing APIs, and compiles the sidecar binary. Desktop frontend development uses Vite, and native packaging uses Rust and Tauri.</p>
    <p>Application services use <code>effect@4.0.0-rc.112</code>. The same exact release is declared across the workspaces that depend on Effect, including the API, database, core, desktop, workflow, and sidecar. Root overrides align the node platform packages to that release. Effect supplies typed errors, schemas, service context, layers, concurrency, and HTTP APIs.</p>
    <p>The code uses the v4 service form <code>Context.Service</code> and HTTP modules under <code>effect/unstable</code>. Version-matched documentation is installed at <code>packages/db/node_modules/effect/ai-docs/src/</code>. Keep dependency upgrades coordinated across consumers.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="data" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Database and contracts</h2>
    <p>Persistence uses <code>drizzle-orm@1.0.0-beta.22</code> with <code>bun:sqlite</code>. Database migration generation uses the matching <code>drizzle-kit</code> release. The schema is TypeScript; generated migration directories contain the SQL and migration metadata used during upgrades.</p>
    <p>Effect HttpApi defines the HTTP contract. The workspace code generator compiles that contract to Promise and Effect client outputs. Zod is also present in API and AI dependencies for integrations that require it; it does not replace HttpApi's boundary schemas.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="desktop" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Desktop and graphics</h2>
    <p>The desktop uses <code>solid-js ^1.9.0</code>, <code>@solidjs/router ^0.15.0</code>, <code>vite ^7.0.0</code>, and Tailwind CSS 3. The workbench uses Kobalte controls, Lucide icons, Monaco editing, and Chart.js where the corresponding features need them.</p>
    <p>The viewport uses <code>three ^0.180.0</code>. Core geometry is separate from rendering; <code>three-csg-ts</code> and <code>occt-import-js 0.0.23</code> support additional desktop geometry/import paths. Tauri v2 supplies native API access, dialogs, shell operations, logging, OS integration, and updater integration through installed plugins.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ai" class="doc-section">
    <h2>AI runtime</h2>
    <p><code>@cadcamfun/ai</code> pins <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next 1.428.0</code> and <code>ai 7.0.127</code>. Direct model adapters include Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenAI-compatible providers, and the AI SDK gateway. The sidecar selects the embedded nikcli host by default or the direct host through <code>CADCAMFUN_AI_HOST</code>.</p>
    <p>Application tools and action schemas sit above provider transport. Changing a provider does not create a second action catalogue. Model connectivity, credentials, cancellation, and session behavior are documented in the <a href="/docs/ai">AI reference</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="clients" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Mobile and documentation</h2>
    <p>The mobile manifest uses Expo <code>~57.0.27</code>, React <code>19.2.3</code>, React Native <code>0.86.3</code>, and Expo Router <code>~57.0.25</code>. Its custom companion transport is a native module. NativeWind, Reanimated, Gesture Handler, SecureStore, and local authentication support the mobile application.</p>
    <p>This site uses Astro <code>^5.14.1</code> with the Cloudflare adapter, MDX and Tailwind integrations. Documentation pages are prerendered; assistant, Markdown, Studio fallback, and artifact routes run on the server. The local Studio API runs in Bun because it uses <code>bun:sqlite</code>.</p>
    <p>Type checking uses Bun's built-in checker, <code>bun check</code>, which reads each workspace's <code>tsconfig.json</code> and reports the same diagnostics as <code>tsc</code>. The manifest ranges describe allowed dependency versions; the lockfile records the installed resolution.</p>
    <p>The documentation site uses Astro 5 with the Cloudflare adapter, MDX, and Tailwind (<code>packages/web/package.json</code>). The desktop webview reaches native code through <code>@tauri-apps/api</code> v2.</p>
  </section>


  <section id="execution-environments" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What runs in each environment</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Code it runs</th><th>Relevant limitation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bun sidecar</td><td>Effect server/services, Drizzle, <code>bun:sqlite</code>, provider clients, workflow and history orchestration.</td><td>A browser or Cloudflare Worker cannot directly substitute for Bun's SQLite runtime.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desktop webview</td><td>SolidJS state and interaction, core geometry, Three.js, API client.</td><td>Native operations require Tauri commands; browser preview supplies a different integration environment.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rust shell</td><td>Tauri lifecycle, native window/menu/tray, secure commands, updater.</td><td>A successful Vite build does not build or validate the native application bundle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expo application</td><td>React Native screens and native companion transport.</td><td>Expo Go lacks the custom pinning module; JavaScript export does not produce a native installer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cloudflare documentation Worker</td><td>Server routes for Markdown, assistant requests, and guide artifacts.</td><td>It has no implicit access to a local sidecar or SQLite file; bindings and backend configuration are explicit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local Studio process</td><td>Bun HTTP server and direct read access through selected Drizzle tables.</td><td>The configured existing database and account session are independent from desktop discovery.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Use the build artifact corresponding to the environment being tested. Frontend output, a compiled sidecar, migration resources, an exported mobile bundle, and signed native installers are distinct outputs even when one command coordinates several stages.</p>
  </section>

