  <section id="why" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Why these choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bun</strong> runs the tests, the scripts and the sidecar, and compiles the sidecar into one native binary, so the desktop ships without a separate JavaScript runtime.</li>
      <li><strong>Effect</strong> gives every service typed errors, scoped resources (the database handle and child processes are released on shutdown) and one HttpApi definition used by server, clients, generated SDKs and the API reference.</li>
      <li><strong>SQLite</strong> keeps each installation self-contained: one file plus a WAL, backed up before each schema upgrade.</li>
      <li><strong>Tauri</strong> uses the system webview and a small Rust shell, which owns the sidecar process, native menus, the updater and the keychain.</li>
      <li><strong>three.js</strong> renders the viewport; the same library draws the 3D previews on these documentation pages.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

